{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the ruler of the County of Tripoli from 1149 to 1177 and regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem from 1172 to 1174. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Helena Irouanna. Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din during the Battle of Inab in 1154 and spent nearly a decade as a prisoner. \n\n*He was released in 1163 and returned to Tripoli where he succeeded his father as count*\n\nRaymond III married Alix daughter of King Fulk III of Jerusalem but the marriage was childless and ended in divorce. He later married Agnes-Maria of Courtenay but this marriage was also childless. Raymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and supported the claim of Hugh IV Count of Saint-James to the throne. \n\n*Raymond III served as regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem after the death of King Amalric I in 1172 until 1174 when he was replaced by Hugh de Lusignan*\n\nRaymond III worked to strengthen the defenses of Tripoli and supported the Knights Templar and Knights Hospitaller. He also formed alliances with the Byzantine Empire and the County of Antioch. Raymond III died in 1177 and was succeeded by his cousin Hugh III Count of Tripoli. \n\n*Raymond IIIs death marked the end of the direct line of Counts of Tripoli descended from Raymond I and led to a period of succession crises in the county* \n\nRaymond III is often portrayed as a wise and experienced leader who worked to maintain the stability of the County of Tripoli during a period of significant turmoil in the region. His role as regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem also highlights his importance in the broader political landscape of the Crusader states. Despite his efforts the County of Tripoli faced significant challenges after his death including conflicts with neighboring states and succession disputes. Raymond IIIs legacy continues to be studied by historians interested in the history of the Crusades and the medieval Middle East. His life and reign provide valuable insights into the complexities of political alliances and the struggles of the Crusader states to maintain power in the region. \n\nAdditional information about Raymond III can be found in various historical sources including the works of medieval historians such as William of Tyre who provided detailed accounts of his life and reign. These sources offer a more detailed understanding of Raymond IIIs actions and decisions as a leader and his impact on the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \n\nThe historical context of Raymond IIIs life is also important to consider including the rise of powerful Muslim leaders such as Nur ad-Din and Saladin who posed significant threats to the Crusader states. Raymond IIIs responses to these threats and his efforts to secure the County of Tripoli are key aspects of his legacy and demonstrate his skills as a military and political leader. \n\nOverall Raymond IIIs life and reign were marked by significant challenges and accomplishments and his impact on the history of the Crusader states remains an important area of study.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -1.2077350616455078}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.5510462364763953e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.965400386136025e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.010501128621399403}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -6.270212179515511e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.7523612768854946e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.421248038648628e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.03552812337875366}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.0917165279388428}, {"token": " ruler", "logprob": -0.861639678478241}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -2.4199192921514623e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.34313344955444336}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.0007651500636711717}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.125986263010418e-06}, {"token": " Tr